  "prompt": "You are an associate analyst in the Industrials & Energy group at Brennan Brothers & Co. Rashida Holm, your managing director, has asked you to prepare a budget-vs-actual variance analysis on Exxon Mobil (XOM) following their Q1 2026 earnings release. She will use this in a client presentation and needs a clean, self-explanatory workbook. Refer to the attached historical financials file for reference context.\n\nBuild the deliverable as an .xlsx workbook with the following structure:\n\n- **Cover sheet**: Company name (Exxon Mobil, XOM), analyst name (Priya Doshi), date, and a confidentiality disclaimer.\n- **Raw Data tab**: Pull Q1 2026 actual figures for Revenues ($85,138M), Net Income ($4,183M), EPS diluted ($1.00), SGA ($2,684M), and Cash ($8,435M) directly from the EDGAR filings cited for the quarter ended 2026-03-31. Include prior-period comparison figures sourced the same way — use the Q2 2023 revenue figure ($164,439M from RevenueFromContractWithCustomerExcludingAssessedTax) and R&D expense ($1,200M from 2025 full year) where relevant to establish baseline context.\n- **Variance tab**: For each line item, calculate dollar variance (Actual − Prior Period) and percentage variance using (Actual − Prior) / |Prior|. Flag any variance exceeding ±10% in absolute terms — these are the ones Rashida will want to discuss with the client.\n- **Summary/Dashboard tab**: An executive summary in investment banking memo format — situation, findings, implications. Highlight the top two material variances by magnitude. Round all figures to the nearest million. Keep this tab presentation-ready; the MD has a 15-minute window and it needs to read cleanly on its own.\n\nFormatting conventions: blue font for hardcoded inputs, black for formulas, green for cross-sheet links. All sheets should have consistent headers and be print-formatted. State clearly which baseline period you're using for the comparison and note that Q1 2026 figures are preliminary and subject to revision.",
  "deliverable": {
    "deliverable_id": "variance_analysis",
    "primary_format": "xlsx",
